.docname {Trust & Service Mesh}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Service mesh | A layer that secures traffic between services, outside their code | Istio |
    | mTLS | Encryption where both sides prove who they are | Between any two pods |
    | Workload identity | The mesh's proof of *which service* is calling | `chat`'s account |
    | ztunnel | Istio's per-node proxy, carries all traffic | One per node |
    | Claims header | Who the person is, set by the edge | `x-gateway-claims` |
    | Forge | Send a made-up header to pose as someone | A fake admin claim |

Who is trusted, by whom, and where that stops.

---

## The idea: a hard shell

| Layer | Question | Where |
|---|---|---|
| Edge | Is this a real signed-in person? | Istio gateway, once |
| Mesh | Is every hop encrypted, and from a real workload? | `ztunnel`, `PeerAuthentication: STRICT` |
| Service | May this person, in this organization, do this? | Cedar, inside the service |

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        K["Keycloak"] --> G["claims-gateway: makes the internal JWT"]
        G --> B["Browser"]
        B --> E["Edge: checks JWT once"]
        E -- "x-gateway-claims" --> S["Service: reads header, runs Cedar"]
        S -- "same header, mTLS" --> S2["Next service"]

Why once: services hold no signing keys, and no request pays for checking twice.

---

## What this does not stop

A workload already inside the cluster can call a service directly and send a forged header. mTLS proves the connection, not the content.

- **From outside:** not possible. Services have no public address, and the gateway wants a JWT.
- **`agent`:** gets extra care, since it runs an LLM on untrusted text. Only the gateway and `chat` may reach it (`k8s/istio-agent-authz.yml`).
- **Accepted for now.**

.box {When this must change} type:{warning}
    This holds while one cluster serves one customer. With several tenants in one hosted cluster, one broken workload becomes a cross-tenant leak. Then each service needs its own `ServiceAccount` and an `AuthorizationPolicy` listing its callers, as `agent` has.

---

## Open doors

| Path | Why open | What guards it |
|---|---|---|
| `/gateway` | `client` has no token before signing in | Keycloak |
| `/telemetry/ingest`, `/collector`, `/observations` | Devices have no person | HMAC with the building's key, in `telemetry` |
| `/agent` | The eval runner has its own token | The edge removes any `x-gateway-claims`, and the policy above |
| `/grafana` | It has its own login | Only that password |
| `/` | The app itself | Nothing needed |

**Why `/agent` strips the header:** the door is open, so nobody checked that header. `agent` would believe a forged one.

---

## Keys

| Between | Key | Note |
|---|---|---|
| Keycloak and `claims-gateway` | OIDC | Only `claims-gateway` talks to Keycloak |
| `claims-gateway` and the edge | RS256, public keys at JWKS | Only `claims-gateway` signs |
| Control plane services | Shared HMAC secret | One implementation, in `auth-contracts` |
| Devices and `telemetry` | HMAC, derived per building | Revoke by bumping an epoch. Separate key: this one leaves for customer sites |

---

## Why Istio, why ambient

- **Istio, not Linkerd:** Linkerd cannot validate a person's JWT. Istio's stable releases are also free.
- **Ambient, not sidecars:** sensors send a lot of data. `ztunnel` only does L4, so nothing parses bulk traffic. Parsing stays at the edge, where decisions are made.

Measured on a single dev node: Redis p50 stays under 5 ms inside the mesh.

---

## Known traps

.box {istiod cannot fetch JWKS from a STRICT pod} type:{warning}
    `istiod` is outside the mesh, so `ztunnel` refuses its plain request. `jwksUri` goes through the gateway's own route instead.

.box {Naming a JWT location replaces the defaults} type:{warning}
    `fromHeaders` or `fromCookies` on a `jwtRule` turns off `Authorization: Bearer`. List both: browsers use the cookie, others the header.

.box {A wildcard misses the bare path} type:{warning}
    Istio's `/twin/*` does not match `/twin`, but the route does. Every protected service is listed in both forms.

.box {Keycloak ignores a changed realm file} type:{warning}
    `--import-realm` skips a realm that exists. After changing `keycloak/realm-export.json`, delete the `keycloak_postgres_data` volume and let it import again.

---

## Local vs cluster

Same gated paths. Local only:

- Vite dev server for the app.
- Mongo Express GUIs at `/*-db-gui`.
- Prometheus through the edge. In the cluster it stays inside.

Simulators are never deployed to the cluster. They post to `/telemetry/ingest` like any device.

---

## Before real production

The manifests give a working cluster. These inputs cannot live in the repository:

| Needed | Today |
|---|---|
| HTTPS | The gateway listens on plain `:80` |
| Keycloak | Not in these manifests |
| Image access | `:latest` with `IfNotPresent`. Use fixed tags |
| Real secrets | `scripts/k8s/k8s-secrets.sh` makes dev ones |
| Model key | `agent` starts without `OPENROUTER_API_KEY`, then fails each request |
| Strong Grafana password | It is the only guard there |
